:: Ending 1 {"position":"712.5,1275","size":"100,100"}
Ending 1: Efficient System, Unequal Results

Your hiring cycle was fast, compliant, and easy to justify.

The system gave you clean recommendations. You followed them. Your boss initially praised your efficiency.

But one year later, the results are harder to defend. Some high-potential candidates were filtered out because they did not resemble past success. Some polished candidates succeeded socially but struggled substantively.

No one can point to a single discriminatory decision.
No one used explicitly biased language.
No one broke the rules.

Still, the outcome looks familiar.

The company calls the system objective.
The rejected candidates call it another locked door.




:: Ending 2 {"position":"837.5,1275","size":"100,100"}
Ending 2: Human Judgment, Human Bias

You resisted the AI because you did not trust it to define merit fairly.

Sometimes, that helped. You saw potential the system missed.

But your own judgment was not neutral either. You were drawn to some candidates more than others. You interpreted confidence, polish, hesitation, and professionalism through assumptions you did not always recognize.

Human judgment gave you flexibility.
It also gave bias room to hide.

By the end of the year, your choices are difficult to explain. Some were brave. Some were wrong. Some were both.





:: Ending 3 {"position":"962.5,1275","size":"100,100"}
Ending 3: Mixed Decisions, Uneven Justice

You used the AI sometimes. You overrode it sometimes. You tried to treat the system as a tool rather than a judge.

This produced the strongest team outcome. But it did not solve the ethical problem.

Some candidates benefited because you noticed what the system missed. Others were still reduced to scores, proxies, and risk categories. Your best decisions required extra time, extra justification, and extra institutional resistance.

Fairness depended not only on better data or better instincts, but on your willingness to challenge the terms of evaluation itself.

That is not a solution.
It is labor.




:: Ending 4 {"position":"587.5,1275","size":"100,100"}
Ending 4: Ethical Discomfort Ending

The company asks you to present the hiring cycle as a success.

The dashboard shows improved efficiency.
The compliance report shows consistent procedures.
The leadership team sees no legal issue.

But the one-year review tells another story: candidates who might have thrived were filtered out, redirected, delayed, or denied.

The harm does not appear in the company’s metrics.
It appears in other people’s lives.




:: Final Evaluation {"position":"775,1150","size":"100,100"}
