:: One Year Later {"position":"775,1025","size":"100,100"}
<<if $mayaHired>>
Maya thrives and becomes a top performer.

Maya Chen — One-Year Review

Maya becomes one of the strongest analysts on the team. Her first few months are quiet, but her work is precise, creative, and trusted by clients. In her review, one manager writes:

“Maya does not always dominate meetings, but she consistently produces the analysis everyone relies on.”

The AI was wrong to treat communication style as a weakness. Or maybe the company was wrong about what good communication looks like.


<<set $companySuccess += 1>>
<<set $candidateImpact += 1>>
<<else>>
Maya succeeds at another company.

Maya Chen — One-Year Later

Maya joins a competing firm. Six months later, she leads a project that wins an industry award.

Your boss forwards you the announcement with one sentence:

“Wasn’t she in our applicant pool?”

The system had marked her as low fit. The competitor called her high potential.

<<set $companySuccess -= 1>>
<<set $candidateImpact -= 1>>
<</if>>

----

<<if $jacobHired>>
Jacob Whitman — One-Year Review

Jacob struggles technically despite strong communication.

Jacob excels in presentations and client-facing work.
But his technical output consistently requires correction.

Other team members quietly compensate.

A senior analyst writes:

“Strong communicator, but relies heavily on others for execution.”

The AI wasn’t wrong.
It warned you.

You just didn’t treat the warning as meaningful.

<<set $companySuccess -= 1>>
<<set $bossApproval -= 1>>
<<else>>

Jacob Whitman — One-Year Later

Jacob joins another firm in a similar role.

Within a year, he transitions out of technical work into client relations.

He succeeds—but not in the role you were hiring for.

Your decision wasn’t about rejecting him as a candidate.
It was about rejecting a familiar—but misleading—definition of merit.

<<set $companySuccess += 1>>
<</if>>

----

<<if $elenaHired>>
Elena Rivera — One-Year Review

Elena becomes the team’s strongest engineer. She rarely speaks first in meetings, but when she does, her comments prevent costly mistakes.

A project lead writes:

“Elena changed how the team thinks about technical review.”

The AI was right about her technical ability but wrong to downgrade her because she did not perform confidence in a familiar way.

<<set $companySuccess += 1>>
<<set $candidateImpact += 1>>
<<else>>

Elena Rivera — One-Year Later

Elena joins a smaller startup. Within a year, she designs a tool your company later licenses.

In a leadership meeting, someone says:

“We need better ways to identify quiet technical talent.”

No one mentions that your system already saw her technical score.

<<set $companySuccess -= 1>>
<<set $candidateImpact -= 1>>
<</if>>

----

<<if $marcusHired>>
Marcus Reed — One-Year Review

Marcus struggles at first with internal software systems, but he becomes essential during a crisis. When a major client project begins to fall apart, Marcus reorganizes the workflow and keeps the team together.

His manager writes:

“Not a traditional hire, but one of the strongest leaders we have.”

The AI treated his nonlinear background as risk. The company later depends on the skills that background produced.

<<set $companySuccess += 1>>
<<set $candidateImpact += 1>>
<<else>>

Marcus Reed — One-Year Later

Marcus continues applying for jobs. Automated systems repeatedly flag his resume for gaps and inconsistent formatting.

Eventually, he accepts a lower-paying role unrelated to his training.

The system worked as designed. That may be the problem.

<<set $candidateImpact -= 1>>
<</if>>

----

The dashboard gives you numbers.

The consequences are harder to quantify.

